                <pb facs="0375" n="275" sameAs="frus:frus1884/pg275"/>

                <head>No. 198.<lb/><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Daggett to Mr.
                    Frelinghuysen.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Legation of the United States</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Honolulu</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1883-12-22"><hi rendition="#italic">December</hi> 22, 1883.</date>
                        (Received January 12.)</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 110.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: I have the honor to inform you that since
                    the issuance of a writ of mandamus restraining the minister of finance from
                    exchanging Hawaiian 6 per cent. bonds for Hawaiian silver coins at their face
                    value, a resolution has been passed by the privy council recognizing such coins
                    as legal tender, “in like manner as silver coins of the United States of like
                    denomination”; and the collector-general of customs has been instructed to
                    accept in the payment of customs duties only United States gold coin or its
                    equivalent. This instruction accords with a law of 1876, which has not been
                    enforced.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer><signed>ROLLIN M. DAGGETT.</signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
